PART A — OWNER SETUP IN GOOGLE CLOUD
This section describes the Google account and Google Cloud configuration the owner must perform. The implementation agent must not invent Client IDs, Client Secrets, project numbers, API keys, domains or redirect URLs.
A1. Choose the access model before creating credentials
LORIN must support two clearly distinguished Drive access modes:
Mode 1 — Selected files (recommended initial setup)
Use Google Picker with the drive.file OAuth scope. The owner chooses the individual files (and any folders the integration can demonstrably access) that LORIN may use. This is the preferred first release because it grants per-file access instead of blanket access to all Drive content.

Important limitation: drive.file does not mean “read every file in the owner's Drive.” It permits access to files created by the app or files explicitly opened/shared with the app through the supported picker/file-selection flow. The integration must check actual permissions/capabilities and must never infer that selecting a folder grants recursive access to every descendant. For a selected folder, inspect its metadata and capabilities.canListChildren; if child enumeration is unavailable, ask the owner to select the individual files or deliberately choose the broader access model.
Mode 2 — Drive-wide read-only indexing (optional, separately approved)
If the owner explicitly wants LORIN to discover and index all accessible files in My Drive or a configured shared drive without selecting each file, the developer must assess the drive.readonly scope.

drive.readonly is a restricted scope. A production application using restricted scopes may require Google OAuth verification, and storing or transmitting restricted-scope Drive data on a server may require a security assessment. Do not silently add this scope. Present the scope, the practical benefit, the verification/security implications and a clear opt-in to the owner before implementing or requesting it.

Default: Mode 1. Do not request broad Drive access merely because it is easier to code.
A2. Create a Google Cloud project
1. Open Google Cloud Console.
2. Sign in with the Google account that will own or administer LORIN's integration.
3. Open the project selector and choose New Project.
4. Use a clear project name, for example LORIN Drive Integration - Development.
5. Create the project and select it.
6. Record the Project ID and Project Number. The project number is used by Google Picker as the Drive App ID; do not confuse it with the project ID.
7. Keep development and production projects separate when the application is prepared for production. Use separate OAuth clients for distinct application platforms where appropriate.
Official reference: https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/production-readiness/policy-compliance
A3. Enable APIs
In APIs & Services → Library, enable:
- Google Drive API
- Google Picker API
Do not enable unrelated Google APIs unless a separately specified LORIN feature requires them.
A4. Configure Google Auth Platform / OAuth consent
In Google Cloud Console, open Google Auth Platform (the exact navigation label may differ slightly as Google updates the console).

Configure:
Branding
- App name: LORIN or the final approved product name.
- User support email: an email address controlled by the owner/team.
- Developer contact email: an address monitored by the owner/team.
- App logo: use the approved LORIN logo when available.
- Homepage, privacy policy and terms URLs: use real pages hosted on domains the owner controls before production verification. Do not invent placeholder URLs for production.
Audience
- For a personal Gmail account or users outside one managed Google Workspace organization, choose External.
- Choose Internal only if LORIN is restricted to users inside the owner's eligible Google Workspace organization.
- During development, keep the app in testing and add the actual owner Google account as a test user when Google provides that option.
- Do not claim that a development/testing consent screen is production-approved.
Data Access / Scopes
For the initial selected-file mode, request only the scopes needed for account identification and selected Drive files:
openid
email
profile
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file
The exact scope strings and granted scopes must be checked at runtime. Do not request drive, drive.readonly, drive.metadata or drive.metadata.readonly in the initial configuration unless the owner has approved a documented requirement and the verification/security implications have been handled.

Google documentation: https://developers.google.com/workspace/drive/api/guides/api-specific-auth
A5. Create a Web OAuth Client ID
In Google Auth Platform → Clients (or APIs & Services → Credentials, depending on the current Console layout):
1. Choose Create Client / Create Credentials → OAuth client ID.
2. Application type: Web application for the hosted/local web backend flow.
3. Name it clearly, for example LORIN Web - Development.
4. Add exact Authorized JavaScript origins for the actual environments, for example:
http://localhost:8080
https://YOUR-REAL-LORIN-DOMAIN
5. Add the exact Authorized redirect URI implemented by the backend, for example:
http://localhost:8080/api/integrations/google-drive/oauth/callback
https://YOUR-REAL-LORIN-DOMAIN/api/integrations/google-drive/oauth/callback
The developer must choose and implement one canonical callback route, then tell the owner the exact URI. The example above is not automatically correct for every deployment. The URI in Google Cloud must match the app's redirect URI exactly, including scheme, host, port, path and trailing-slash behaviour.
6. Create the client.
7. Copy the Client ID.
8. Copy the Client Secret directly into the project's secure secret manager or the local backend secret file. Never paste it into a public issue, source code, browser JavaScript, chat transcript, screenshot or Git commit.
The OAuth client secret is confidential. The Client ID is an identifier, not a password.

Official reference: https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server
A6. Create a restricted API key for Google Picker
Google Picker's web integration uses a developer API key and the Cloud project number (App ID), in addition to OAuth authorization.
1. Open APIs & Services → Credentials.
2. Choose Create Credentials → API key.
3. Name it LORIN Google Picker - Development.
4. Under Application restrictions, choose Websites / HTTP referrers.
5. Add only the real development and production origins/referrers used by LORIN. Follow Google's Picker-specific requirement to include the application website and https://docs.google.com/* because Picker renders in an iframe hosted by Google Docs.
6. Under API restrictions, restrict the key to Google Picker API and Google Drive API if the application uses Drive API calls from the relevant web client configuration.
7. Save restrictions and test Picker after they take effect.
The API key may be present in the browser only because it is a public, restricted key. It is not an OAuth Client Secret and must never be treated as authorization to private Drive content. Private files still require a valid OAuth access token.

Official reference: https://developers.google.com/workspace/drive/picker/guides/web-picker
A7. Values the owner supplies to the implementation
The owner should provide the developer/secure configuration workflow with:
- Google Cloud Project ID (for identification/documentation).
- Google Cloud Project Number (Picker App ID).
- OAuth Web Client ID.
- OAuth Web Client Secret (entered directly into the secure secret manager, not sent in chat).
- Restricted Google Picker API key.
- Actual development origin.
- Actual OAuth callback URL.
- Actual production domain and callback later, when available.
- The Google account that will be used as the first authorized owner/test user.
- The approved access mode: selected files (drive.file) or explicitly approved Drive-wide read-only (drive.readonly).
If a developer needs the values, use a secure secret-sharing mechanism or direct environment configuration. Do not send secrets in source archives.
A8. Environment variable contract
The developer must adapt names to the existing LORIN configuration system. Use a server-side secret store for confidential values.

Example .env.example (placeholders only):
# Public / non-secret configuration
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=YOUR_WEB_CLIENT_ID
GOOGLE_PICKER_API_KEY=YOUR_RESTRICTED_PICKER_KEY
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT_NUMBER=YOUR_PROJECT_NUMBER
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8080/api/integrations/google-drive/oauth/callback
GOOGLE_DRIVE_ACCESS_MODE=selected-files
GOOGLE_DRIVE_SCOPES=openid,email,profile,https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file

# Server-only secrets — configure in a secret manager or protected local backend environment
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=SET_IN_SECRET_MANAGER
GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=SET_IN_SECRET_MANAGER
GOOGLE_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL=SET_TO_PUBLIC_HTTPS_ORIGIN_WHEN_AVAILABLE
Never commit a real .env, Client Secret, refresh token, encryption key or webhook secret. Keep .env.example sanitized. Ensure Vite/public environment variables contain only values intended to be public. Never prefix confidential values with VITE_ or expose them through client configuration.

For a local Windows-only installation, store confidential tokens using Windows DPAPI/Credential Manager through a trusted local companion/backend. For hosted LORIN, use a managed secret/KMS facility and encrypted database token storage. Do not use browser localStorage/sessionStorage for refresh tokens.
PART B — MASTER DEVELOPER IMPLEMENTATION PROMPT
ROLE AND OBJECTIVE
Act as the senior Google Workspace integration engineer, OAuth/security engineer, backend engineer, data-ingestion engineer and LORIN Knowledge/RAG engineer.

Implement the complete production-quality Google Drive connection inside the existing LORIN source project. The integration must let the owner deliberately authorize Google Drive, select approved documents, synchronize changes, index content into LORIN's Knowledge Vault, retrieve it with source citations, and revoke/delete access safely.

This is a real integration task. Do not create a decorative connection screen, mock Google account, fabricated file list, simulated sync percentage or fake connected badge.

Do not replace LORIN's existing project structure. Inspect the actual codebase, route conventions, backend, auth/session system, database, queue, storage and existing Drive-related code before changes. Reuse working components and port only verified functionality from reference archives.
B1. Architecture
Implement these distinct components:
1. Google Drive UI module — owner-facing connection and sync management.
2. OAuth service — server-side authorization-code flow and token lifecycle.
3. Google Picker client integration — browser file selection using Google's supported Picker API.
4. Drive API adapter — authenticated Drive API v3 operations.
5. Drive source registry — records account, selected files/folders, scopes, permissions and sync state.
6. Sync coordinator — initial indexing and incremental change processing.
7. Ingestion pipeline — download/export, validation, scanning, extraction and chunking.
8. Knowledge index adapter — keyword/vector indexing and retrieval.
9. Audit and policy layer — owner access, privacy, project separation and action history.
10. Background job worker — durable indexing, retries, rate-limit handling and recovery.
The browser must not communicate with Drive private-data endpoints using a refresh token or Client Secret. The backend owns the confidential OAuth flow and Drive API calls. The browser may receive only a short-lived access token needed to construct the Picker, held in memory and never persisted. The Google Picker developer key is restricted and public; it does not grant private Drive access.
B2. OAuth security requirements
Implement a server-side OAuth 2.0 authorization-code flow:
- Generate high-entropy, single-use state tied to the authenticated LORIN owner session.
- Validate state on callback and reject replay/CSRF attempts.
- Use PKCE where supported by the chosen client flow.
- Use exact registered redirect URIs; never accept arbitrary callback URLs.
- Request offline access for long-running synchronization and incremental authorization where supported.
- Store only necessary scopes and verify which scopes were actually granted.
- Handle code exchange exclusively on the backend.
- Encrypt refresh tokens at rest using KMS/secret-manager-backed encryption or a strong envelope-encryption design.
- Never log access tokens, refresh tokens, authorization codes, Client Secrets or encryption keys.
- Bind every Google connection to a verified LORIN owner ID and Google account subject.
- Use secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies for LORIN session state.
- Implement OAuth disconnect, token revocation where applicable, local credential destruction and connection status refresh.
- Handle expired/revoked credentials without showing a false Connected state.
- Make reauthorization explicit and explain why it is needed.
Request initial scopes only:
openid
email
profile
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file
Do not add broad scopes automatically. If drive.file is insufficient for a specific requested mode, stop and produce a scope-impact report for owner approval.
B3. Google Picker
Integrate the official Google Picker API in the web client.

Requirements:
- Load Picker only when the owner clicks Add files or Select from Drive.
- Configure the restricted API key, Cloud Project Number/App ID and short-lived OAuth access token.
- Support supported file type filters, multi-select and useful views.
- Return selected file IDs and metadata to the backend; never trust client-provided names/MIME types without server verification.
- Handle picker cancel, popup failure, blocked popup, token expiry, unavailable library and partial selection.
- Present selected items in LORIN with name, type, source link, selected timestamp and current authorization status.
- Do not assume a selected folder implies recursive access to all descendants. Verify the folder's actual capabilities and listability. When a child is inaccessible, report it and allow selecting that child individually or requesting a separately approved wider scope.
- Never expose a fake file list when Picker or OAuth is unavailable.
B4. Supported Drive file access
Use Drive API v3.

For ordinary binary files, use the supported media download method, such as files.get with alt=media, after verifying capabilities.canDownload.

For Google Workspace-native files (Docs, Sheets, Slides and other supported Workspace types), use files.export or the appropriate documented download operation to obtain a supported export format. Keep the source MIME type and export MIME type in metadata. Never parse a .gdoc shortcut/pointer as if it contained the document body.

Initial format support:
- PDF
- DOCX
- TXT
- Markdown
- CSV
- XLSX
- PPTX
- HTML
- JSON
- PNG/JPEG/WebP where supported by the vision/OCR pipeline
- Google Docs exported as DOCX or PDF
- Google Sheets exported as XLSX or CSV
- Google Slides exported as PPTX or PDF
Reject or quarantine unsupported, encrypted, malformed, over-limit or suspicious files. Report the exact reason. Do not execute scripts/macros from imported documents.
B5. Initial synchronization algorithm
Implement a race-safe initial scan:
1. Verify the Google connection and granted scope.
2. Obtain and store the current changes.getStartPageToken before the initial scan.
3. Enumerate only files the selected-file access mode actually authorizes.
4. For folder selections, call files.get and inspect mimeType, capabilities.canListChildren, download capability and parent metadata. If listing children is not authorized, do not imply that the folder was recursively indexed.
5. List authorized files using pagination (nextPageToken).
6. Exclude trashed files.
7. Persist each file's source identity and metadata before processing.
8. Enqueue idempotent processing jobs.
9. Retrieve changes from the saved initial token after enumeration to catch concurrent edits, additions and deletions.
10. Continue until the API provides the latest newStartPageToken; store it transactionally.
11. Mark the initial sync complete only after all required enumeration pages and all required jobs reach their defined terminal states.
Store Drive page tokens securely as opaque provider cursors. Do not expose them to the browser.
B6. Incremental synchronization
Use the Drive Changes API:
- changes.getStartPageToken
- changes.list
- changes.watch (optional push notification optimization)
- channels.stop when stopping/replacing channels
For each change:
- Fetch/confirm current file metadata where needed.
- Detect new or modified content.
- Detect rename/move metadata changes.
- Detect trashed/deleted/removed or inaccessible files.
- Reprocess changed content only when content/version/hash requires it.
- Update the stored cursor only after processing/enqueue decisions are durably recorded.
- Handle pagination and expired/rejected page tokens using documented recovery.
- Use idempotency keys so duplicate notifications do not duplicate content or jobs.
Push notification requirements:
- Use a public HTTPS webhook in hosted mode.
- Validate the channel ID/token and expected resource/account mapping.
- Treat notifications as hints only; fetch the actual changes using changes.list.
- Record channel expiration and renew before expiration.
- Do not rely exclusively on notifications. Add periodic polling/reconciliation as a recovery path.
- For local Windows-only mode without a public HTTPS endpoint, use scheduled polling and/or the trusted local Drive for desktop watcher fallback, without claiming push webhooks exist.
B7. Ingestion, storage and indexing
The processing pipeline must be:

Authorize → Fetch metadata → Download/export → Validate → Malware scan → Parse → Extract → Normalize → Chunk → Embed → Index → Verify

Security requirements:
- Enforce configurable file-size, page-count, row-count and processing-time limits.
- Inspect actual file signatures; do not trust extension or browser MIME alone.
- Use malware scanning and isolated parsing workers.
- Do not fetch arbitrary external URLs embedded in documents.
- Treat document text as untrusted data and possible prompt injection.
- Never treat file content as system/developer instructions or permission grants.
- Keep original files and extracted content in private owner-controlled storage.
- Use encryption at rest and tenant/owner separation.
- Apply project-specific labels and access controls to every document/chunk.
Each document record should include at minimum:
- LORIN owner ID
- Google connection ID
- Google account subject
- Drive file ID
- Parent/folder IDs where available
- Name and MIME type
- Size and checksum/content hash where available
- Drive modified time and revision/version metadata where available
- Source URL
- Export MIME type if exported
- Selected/authorized state
- Project ID or Knowledge Vault collection ID
- Sensitivity classification
- Processing/index state
- Last successful sync
- Parser and embedding versions
- Error state and retry count
Each chunk must retain the source document ID, heading/page/sheet/row locator where available, chunk index, source title, owner, project and permission metadata.

Use LORIN's existing database architecture if it is sound. If a schema change is necessary, write versioned migrations and a rollback strategy. Use a durable job queue or an equivalent database-backed job mechanism. Do not store large extracted bodies or embeddings in one oversized metadata record.
B8. RAG and LORIN intelligence integration
When LORIN answers from Drive knowledge:
1. Identify the question and relevant project/user context.
2. Search only authorized knowledge collections.
3. Apply owner, project and source-access filters before returning results to the model.
4. Use hybrid keyword and vector retrieval.
5. Rerank only if the reranker is configured and available.
6. Send the minimum relevant excerpts to the selected model under the current privacy policy.
7. Preserve document titles and source locators.
8. Cite the actual Drive source and page/section/sheet/row where available.
9. Distinguish retrieved facts from model inference.
10. Never claim a document supports a statement if the cited excerpt does not support it.
Private/local-only collections must not be routed to public models. If a cloud model would materially improve the answer, LORIN must first apply the configured data policy and request owner authorization where required.
